{:edb/db-before-t 2676 :edb/db-after-t 2677 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535989 50 #inst "2026-10-09T12:21:39.837Z" 13194139535989 true] [17592186184291 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378403 13194139535989 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378410 13194139535989 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378413 13194139535989 true] [17592186184292 1020 #edb/ref 17592186378417 13194139535989 true] [17592186378403 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378404 13194139535989 true] [17592186378403 1089 "q-126847" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378403 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535989 true] [17592186378403 1100 "Find the first three non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ about the point $x=1.$" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378403 1113 #uuid "533de05b-1751-5888-841c-ffc1b54b5265" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378403 1145 "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{‴}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x \\\\ f(1) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1=\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{1+1}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\frac{2x}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)}{(1+1)^{2}}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+(\\frac{1}{2})(x-1)+\\frac{1}{2!}(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)^{2}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378405 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378406 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378407 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378408 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378409 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1041 "selection" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378406 13194139535989 true] [17592186378404 1110 #uuid "a8ac5c05-26f8-59ba-bc15-97f822d699f2" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378405 1034 "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x-1)+\\frac{\\pi }{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378405 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535989 true] [17592186378405 1111 #uuid "815187de-f4ee-5812-b0ab-d424fbc39b56" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378406 1034 "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{4}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378406 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535989 true] [17592186378406 1111 #uuid "df3a993c-291b-5fa8-8dad-4750737b3e4c" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378407 1034 "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(x-1)-\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378407 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535989 true] [17592186378407 1111 #uuid "7b9dd9ca-9ddf-5faf-b965-79138306241e" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378408 1034 "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+(x-1)-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378408 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535989 true] [17592186378408 1111 #uuid "8a7f4989-212a-516f-b5ce-6349d74852bc" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378409 1034 "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)-\\frac{1}{8}(x-1)^{2}+⋯" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378409 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535989 true] [17592186378409 1111 #uuid "3f6d76f9-dcca-5cbb-892e-06ae5faf47e3" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378410 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378411 13194139535989 true] [17592186378410 1089 "q-354017" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378410 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535989 true] [17592186378410 1100 "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ about $x=2\\pi $ to approximate the value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5).$ Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378410 1113 #uuid "6d5cd0a4-e6bd-5a8c-a006-38f56f18a060" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378410 1145 "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $a=2\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(2\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{‴}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{‴}(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =(1)+(0)(x-2\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-2\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(0)}{3!}(x-2\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{(1)}{4!}(x-2\\pi )^{4}+⋯ \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(x-2\\pi )^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-2\\pi )^{4}}{24}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5) & ≈1-\\frac{(5-2\\pi )^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(5-2\\pi )^{4}}{24} \\\\  & ≈0.29,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535989 true] [17592186378411 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378412 13194139535989 true] [17592186378411 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535989 true] [17592186378411 1041 "field-1" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378411 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378412 13194139535989 true] [17592186378411 1110 #uuid "c358405a-917e-5b3c-a016-214889b2a28a" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378412 1034 "0.29" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378412 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535989 true] [17592186378412 1111 #uuid "594cbfef-f66b-5359-ac46-8f0a3dcc5b10" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378413 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378414 13194139535989 true] [17592186378413 1089 "q-363485" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378413 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535989 true] [17592186378413 1100 "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $f(x)={2}^{x}$ about $x=2$ to approximate the value of ${2}^{2.1}.$ Round your answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n${2}^{2.1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378413 1113 #uuid "79c3e8b3-1afe-5d55-adc2-24f3988011bf" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378413 1145 "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $a=2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x} \\\\ f(2) & =4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={2}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor expansion is\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}=4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(x-2)+\\frac{4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}}{2!}(x-2)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n{2}^{x}=4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(x-2)+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}(x-2)^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=2.1.$ Since $2.1-2=0.1=\\frac{1}{10},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{2.1} & ≈4+4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)(\\frac{1}{10})+2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈4.29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{2}^{2.1}≈4.29.\n$$" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378415 13194139535989 true] [17592186378414 1000 #edb/ref 17592186378416 13194139535989 true] [17592186378414 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535989 true] [17592186378414 1041 "field-1" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378414 1104 #edb/ref 17592186378416 13194139535989 true] [17592186378414 1110 #uuid "9ea677ed-5f99-53d5-8a64-9f8e64a9264a" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378415 1034 "4.28" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378415 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535989 true] [17592186378415 1111 #uuid "15a7d1ce-086b-55d8-bba1-d874a9bc04ec" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378416 1034 "4.29" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378416 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535989 true] [17592186378416 1111 #uuid "90dfbe70-e064-54fa-9ef8-5b545809f99c" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378417 1017 #edb/ref 17592186378418 13194139535989 true] [17592186378417 1089 "q-363486" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378417 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535989 true] [17592186378417 1100 "Use the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of $f(x)={5}^{x}$ about $x=1$ to approximate the value of ${5}^{1.3}.$ Round your answer to **one decimal place**.\n\n${5}^{1.3}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378417 1113 #uuid "52462734-7b5e-5643-af09-a9a3063c6148" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378417 1145 "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)={5}^{x}$ and $a=1.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={5}^{x} \\\\ f(1) & =5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={5}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor expansion is\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}=5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(x-1)+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n{5}^{x}=5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(x-1)+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2}(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=1.3.$ Since $1.3-1=0.3=\\frac{3}{10},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{1.3} & ≈5+5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)(\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{5(\\operatorname{ln}⁡5)^{2}}{2}{(\\frac{3}{10})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈8.0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{5}^{1.3}≈8.0.\n$$" 13194139535989 true] [17592186378418 1000 #edb/re